    },
    {
      "id": "R11",
      "met": "partial",
      "where": "Bibliography"
    }
  ],
  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "A sun-synchronous orbit does not provide a constant supply of sunlight to the spacecraft; it experiences eclipses depending on orbital geometry and season."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The claim that the selected Tasmanian station provides near-polar access frequency is overstated. At about 42°S it is not close to a pole, and access frequency depends on station latitude, orbit geometry, antenna elevation mask, and the analysed time span."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The claim that imaging occurs on the descending part because it is constantly in sunlight is not generally valid. SSO local solar time and ascending/descending illumination must be specified; either branch may be sunlit depending on the orbit."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The orbital-element table does not fully define the initial spacecraft state: for a circular orbit, argument of perigee and true anomaly are undefined, but argument of latitude alone is insufficient without a reference epoch and the remaining element values such as RAAN and inclination."
    },
    {
      "where": "Launch",
      "issue": "The report presents Vega E as the selected launch vehicle despite describing it as not yet built, and does not establish that its payload interface, launch date, injection orbit, or rideshare availability suit this mission."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "A drag sail can increase atmospheric drag but does not itself guarantee a safe disposal time or complete demise. The report gives no sail area, spacecraft ballistic coefficient, decay-time estimate, or assessment against applicable debris-mitigation limits."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The stated 250 km reef width and 600 km altitude are not connected to sensor swath, ground sampling distance, or revisit/coverage calculations, so the claimed imaging capability is unsubstantiated."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "The report proposes a relevant Australian reef-monitoring mission and makes useful use of STK screenshots and access analysis. Its orbit and mission outline are promising, but launch feasibility, imaging performance, communications assumptions, and disposal feasibility are not adequately demonstrated. Presentation and referencing also need improvement; no STK project files are included."
}
